From: Xun2202 <98207886+Xun2202@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 14:38:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] app: scroll recorder counts dropped reports, stages and cover
 loads
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A browse-grid fling can keep Choreographer at 90 Hz while FrameMetrics
callbacks fall to about 60% of those ticks, and every reported frame is
still under its deadline. The listener ignored dropCountSinceLastInvocation,
and stage times were kept only for jank frames, so a stuffed swap chain
could not be told apart from a listener that dropped reports.

HarmonyFramePerf (still the file copied byte for byte into the sibling
app) now stores dropCountSinceLastInvocation on each frame. The listener
runs on HandlerThread "HarmonyFramePerf" at THREAD_PRIORITY_DISPLAY and
only copies primitives into the ring. The 500 ms sampler moved to
"HarmonyFramePerfSampler", so a /proc read cannot stall delivery and
inflate that drop count.

Each scroll segment, and the whole recording, reports p50/p90 of draw,
sync, command issue, swap, gpu and layout for every frame, plus reported
frames, the sum of dropCount, Choreographer ticks, and missed vsyncs.
A missed vsync is a gap between consecutive intended vsyncs that is
neither a report nor a dropped report:
max(0, round(delta / refreshPeriod) - 1 - dropCount). The period is the
display refresh sample in effect, not FrameMetrics.DEADLINE, which on a
90 Hz panel is often 16.7 ms or about 21 ms. The last 30 s also has one
line per second (ticks, frames, drops, missed, dominant phase, and
swap / sync / gpu p90).

Each sample adds Java heap, native heap, art.gc.gc-time and
blocking-gc-count deltas, per-thread CPU (main, RenderThread, hwui,
DefaultDispatcher, OkHttp, the recorder threads, other), how long the
sample took, and Coil counts (DataSource, hardware bitmaps, errors,
starts). The Coil listener is registered on the image loader and does
nothing but a volatile check while recording is off. A failed per-thread
read sticks and is printed as n/a. BLASTBufferQueue lines are split into
"max frames", "Faking releaseBufferCallback" and other; only counts are
exported, and the crash-log filter still matches the whole tag.

Long-press on the "记录" button cycles 封面淡入, 硬件位图 and 预取, and
writes "mark toggle <describe()>" into the scroll trace so one recording
can be split by arm. Tap still exports. The header stores the toggle
state at start and at export.

The switches live in HarmonyPerfToggles (also shared byte for byte), in
memory only, so a restart is back on crossfade=on hardware=on
prefetch=bounds.

The export format is harmony-scroll-perf 2.

